---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
---

<Base
  title="CoreAI · Kham Udom"
  description="Two projects on Microsoft CoreAI: the CoreAI Design System for Azure Developer Services portals, and vsc-ui-react for the Foundry Toolkit."
>
  <main id="main">
    <section class="case-page" id="case-coreai" aria-label="Project: CoreAI">
      <div class="wrap" style="padding-block: clamp(2.5rem, 6vw, 4rem);">
        <ch-breadcrumb label="Breadcrumb">
          <ch-breadcrumb-item href="/">kham.udom</ch-breadcrumb-item>
          <ch-breadcrumb-item href="/#work">work</ch-breadcrumb-item>
          <ch-breadcrumb-item current="page">coreai</ch-breadcrumb-item>
        </ch-breadcrumb>

        <div class="case-head">
          <div>
            <h1 tabindex="-1" class="case-title">CoreAI: design system &amp; toolkit UI</h1>
            <div class="chips">
              <ch-badge>Fluent UI</ch-badge><ch-badge>React</ch-badge><ch-badge>Storybook</ch-badge><ch-badge>MCP</ch-badge><ch-badge>Design systems</ch-badge>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="case-actions">
            <ch-button
              class="kh-btn-primary"
              href="https://commandline.microsoft.com/design-product-simplicity-seamless-ux-ui-agentic-ai-azure-developer-services-foundry/"
              target="_blank">Microsoft article ↗</ch-button
            >
            <ch-button class="kh-btn-ghost" href="https://github.com/vuxhub/vsc-ui-react" target="_blank"
              >vsc-ui-react ↗</ch-button
            >
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="case-section" style="margin-top: 2rem;">
          <p>
            <strong>The team.</strong> CoreAI is Microsoft's organization for AI
            platforms, developer tools, and services, including Microsoft Foundry
            and Azure Developer Services. On that team I worked on two related
            projects: the <strong>CoreAI Design System</strong> for internal Azure
            Developer Services and related Azure Dev portal apps, and
            <strong>vsc-ui-react</strong> for the Foundry Toolkit extension.
            Accessibility is part of both.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="shot">
          <figure>
            <div class="media-frame">
              <img
                src="/images/foundry.jpg"
                alt="Microsoft Foundry landing page with the headline The AI app and agent factory"
                loading="lazy"
              />
            </div>
            <figcaption>
              fig 1. Microsoft Foundry, one of the CoreAI product surfaces this work
              supports.
            </figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>

        <div class="case-section">
          <h2>1. CoreAI Design System</h2>
          <p>
            Azure Developer Services and related portals already used Fluent 2 /
            Fluent UI components, but the products still felt like separate
            houses built from the same bricks: different navigation, create
            flows, tables, and detail pages. The CoreAI Design System is the
            shared layer that gives those portals common foundations without
            forcing every product to look identical.
          </p>
          <p>
            It builds on the Foundry team's design-system work and targets the
            Azure-facing portals under CoreAI. The delivery model is for people
            and for agents: a Figma library for designers working in the usual
            handoff, and an MCP so coding agents and AI-assisted workflows can
            pull the same getting-started guidance, usage examples, and pattern
            documentation. I contribute to the React theme package, Storybook,
            and that MCP. The theme package and MCP are internal; they are not
            open source.
          </p>
          <p>
            I was part of the cross-team v-team of designers and design
            engineers from Foundry and Azure Developer Services that continues to
            formalize high-impact pattern guidance for the system. Microsoft has
            written about that work in
            <a
              href="https://commandline.microsoft.com/design-product-simplicity-seamless-ux-ui-agentic-ai-azure-developer-services-foundry/"
              target="_blank"
              rel="noopener">Designing product simplicity for the AI era</a
            >, which names me among the contributors.
          </p>
          <ol class="flow-steps">
            <li>
              <span
                ><strong>Problem.</strong> Shared Fluent components were not enough.
                Independently built portals still reinvented the same product
                patterns, which customers experience as complexity.</span
              >
            </li>
            <li>
              <span
                ><strong>System.</strong> A CoreAI-wide framework on Fluent: theme,
                Storybook, Figma library, and MCP, so humans and agents start from
                the same foundations.</span
              >
            </li>
            <li>
              <span
                ><strong>Adoption.</strong> Azure portal teams can install the
                baseline npm package in a few days when repo access and PR review
                are available. Public write-up: none of those portals used the
                package in June 2026; by August more than five had it installed.</span
              >
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <div class="case-section">
          <h2>2. vsc-ui-react for Foundry Toolkit</h2>
          <p>
            Separately, I work with the Foundry Toolkit extension team on
            <a href="https://github.com/vuxhub/vsc-ui-react" target="_blank" rel="noopener"
              ><code>vsc-ui-react</code></a
            >, the public VS Code-styled Fluent UI library that ships inside that
            extension (1.2M+ installs). It wraps Fluent UI v9 with Griffel and
            <code>--vscode-*</code> theme tokens so the extension picks up the
            editor's light and dark theme. TypeScript, Vite, Storybook, and a
            <code>prepublishOnly</code> gate of build, typecheck, lint, and Vitest.
          </p>
          <p>
            My public commits, authored as
            <a
              href="https://github.com/vuxhub/vsc-ui-react/commits?author=khamudom"
              target="_blank"
              rel="noopener">khamudom</a
            >, add components on top of Fluent and revise ones already in the set:
          </p>
          <ol class="flow-steps">
            <li>
              <span
                ><strong>New wrappers.</strong> Checkbox, Switch, Dialog, Link,
                SpinButton, Popover, MessageBar, Slider, Badge, Accordion, Divider,
                Breadcrumb, and Tag. Label was co-authored.</span
              >
            </li>
            <li>
              <span
                ><strong>API on components already there.</strong> SearchBox gained
                outline and transparent appearances. TabList gained a primary
                appearance, then the accent was limited to the selected tab. Button
                gained a compact height adjustment.</span
              >
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <div class="case-section">
          <h2>Decisions in vsc-ui-react</h2>
          <div class="principles" style="margin-top: 1.5rem;">
            <ch-card>
              <span class="k">decision.api</span>
              <h3>The wrapper owns the prop</h3>
              <p>
                <code>VscSpinButton</code> types <code>readOnly</code> on its own
                props, pulls it out of the rest object so it does not land on
                Fluent's root, and passes it to the input slot. A consumer-supplied
                input slot is merged with that, so the read-only style and the
                caller's slot can both apply.
              </p>
            </ch-card>
            <ch-card>
              <span class="k">decision.focus</span>
              <h3>Focus trap changes how the example closes</h3>
              <p>
                <code>VscPopover</code> builds on Fluent's Popover, with default and
                brand surfaces from <code>--vscode-*</code> tokens.
                <code>trapFocus</code> blocks outside-click dismissal, so the
                Storybook and playground examples are controlled: Cancel and Save
                call <code>setOpen(false)</code>. Those surfaces get accessible
                names. Link and TabList commits include <code>:focus-visible</code>
                treatment, and the primary tab accent stays on the selected tab when
                it is focused.
              </p>
            </ch-card>
            <ch-card>
              <span class="k">decision.theme</span>
              <h3>VS Code tokens, not a forked palette</h3>
              <p>
                Surfaces, type, and validation states read the editor's theme
                variables. The brand popover arrow inherits the surface background,
                which computes to <code>--vscode-button-background</code>. Motion on
                the popover surface is disabled in the base styles to match VS Code,
                and the prop that would turn it back on is a no-op on purpose.
              </p>
            </ch-card>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="case-section">
          <h2>Where it ships</h2>
          <p>
            The CoreAI Design System ships into Azure Developer Services and
            related Azure Dev portal apps through the internal npm package,
            Storybook, Figma library, and MCP. <code>vsc-ui-react</code> ships in
            the Foundry Toolkit on the Visual Studio Marketplace.
          </p>
          <div class="card-links">
            <a
              href="https://commandline.microsoft.com/design-product-simplicity-seamless-ux-ui-agentic-ai-azure-developer-services-foundry/"
              target="_blank"
              rel="noopener">Microsoft article ↗</a
            >
            <a href="https://ai.azure.com/home" target="_blank" rel="noopener"
              >Microsoft Foundry ↗</a
            >
            <a
              href="https://marketplace.visualstudio.com/items?itemName=ms-windows-ai-studio.windows-ai-studio"
              target="_blank"
              rel="noopener">Foundry Toolkit ↗</a
            >
            <a href="https://www.npmjs.com/package/vsc-ui-react" target="_blank" rel="noopener"
              >npm vsc-ui-react ↗</a
            >
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="case-section">
          <h2>What transfers</h2>
          <p>
            On CoreAI I help build the design system that keeps related Azure Dev
            portals coherent, including the theme, Storybook, and MCP path so
            people and agents use the same foundations. On the Toolkit side I put
            VS Code theme tokens on Fluent, keep wrapper APIs honest, and ship
            those components with Storybook and tests.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="case-footer-nav">
          <ch-button class="kh-btn-ghost" href="/#work">← Back to all work</ch-button>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>
</Base>
